<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ewuare II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> II is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, crowned in 2016 in succession to his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erediauwa">Erediauwa</a>. Before his accession he was a Nigerian diplomat, serving as ambassador to several countries. As traditional ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo people</a> he has pressed for the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> looted by British forces in 1897, a number of which have since been handed back.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ewuare-ii.jpg" alt="Ewuare II" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OTA11 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igue_festival_(%E1%BB%8Cba_of_Benin).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
